FutexSpinTest: measure with steady_clock, and give the timed cases a cross-clock tolerance.

`FutexSpin-Timed-{8,16,32,64}bit` arm a 250 ms deadline through SpinWaitLock::Wait and then REQUIRE that
the elapsed wait, measured with std::chrono::high_resolution_clock, was at least 250 ms. Two distinct
defects hide in that comparison, and on a stock GitHub aarch64 runner they produced a check-phase
failure in which three of the four cases failed while the fourth passed in the very same run — same
binary, same machine, per-case marginality deciding each one.

1. THE MEASURING CLOCK CAN STEP. Under libstdc++, high_resolution_clock is an alias for system_clock,
   i.e. CLOCK_REALTIME (this build links libstdc++: ENABLE_LIBCXX defaults FALSE and the produced
   binaries carry DT_NEEDED libstdc++.so.6). Measuring a duration with the realtime clock is wrong
   regardless of any tolerance: NTP/chrony steps it, and a backward step inside the window shortens the
   measured wait by an arbitrary amount — CI machines are fresh VMs whose clocks are being actively
   disciplined. Every measurement in this file moves to steady_clock, which never steps. This also makes
   FutexSpin-Sleep-8bit sound as-is: its 1 s reference is this_thread::sleep_for, which sleeps on the
   monotonic clock, so reference and measurement now use the same clock and its bare >= needs no slack.

2. THE TIMED DEADLINE IS ARMED ON A DIFFERENT CLOCK THAN THE ONE CHECKING IT. SpinWaitLock converts the
   request into cycle-counter ticks:

       TimeoutCycles = 250e6 ns / (1'000'000'000 / CNTFRQ_EL0)      <- both divisions integer

   Wherever the counter frequency divides 1e9 exactly — 25/50/100 MHz parts, and the 1 GHz that ARMv8.6
   mandates, i.e. essentially every server ARM — the conversion is exact: the wait returns after exactly
   250 ms of CNTVCT_EL0 time, and the REQUIRE holds only if the measuring clock ran no slower than the
   counter over the window. CLOCK_MONOTONIC is NTP-slewed by up to 500 ppm, so it can legitimately
   measure that span as 249.875 ms: exact-or-fail. A part whose frequency does NOT divide evenly passes
   only by accident — Apple's 24 MHz (measured) truncates 41.667 ns/cycle to 41, overshooting every wait
   by 1.6%, which is why this never reproduces on an Asahi dev box.

   The timed cases therefore allow 2 ms of slack: ~16x the worst legal slew over the window, while a
   genuine early return — the property these cases exist to check — still fails by two orders of
   magnitude.

Note what is NOT wrong here: the emulator. ConvertNanosecondsToCycles errs long or exact on every
frequency, never short, so a real wait is never cut off early. The defects are entirely in the test's
choice of measuring clock and its zero-tolerance comparison across clock domains.

--- a/FEXCore/unittests/APITests/FutexSpinTest.cpp	2026-09-20 22:12:49.206418593 -0400
+++ b/FEXCore/unittests/APITests/FutexSpinTest.cpp	2026-09-20 22:12:49.258418035 -0400
@@ -6,16 +6,31 @@
 
 constexpr auto SleepAmount = std::chrono::milliseconds(250);
 
+// The timed cases below arm their deadline in CYCLE-COUNTER ticks (SpinWaitLock converts the request
+// via TimeoutCycles = ns / (1'000'000'000 / CNTFRQ_EL0), both divisions integer) and then measure the
+// elapsed wait with std::chrono — a DIFFERENT clock, so the comparison needs slack for the rate offset
+// between the two. Wherever the counter frequency divides 1e9 evenly (25/50/100 MHz parts, and the 1GHz
+// that ARMv8.6 mandates) the conversion is exact, the wait is exactly the requested span of counter
+// time, and a monotonic clock running NTP-slow by even a fraction of a ppm measures it short: the
+// REQUIRE below is then exact-or-fail. (A part whose frequency does NOT divide evenly passes only by
+// luck — 24MHz truncates 41.667 ns/cycle to 41 and overshoots every wait by 1.6%.)
+//
+// 2ms of slack is ~16x the worst NTP slew (500ppm over 250ms = 125us) yet still fails a genuine early
+// return — the property these cases exist to check — by two orders of magnitude.
+constexpr auto SleepTolerance = std::chrono::milliseconds(2);
+
 TEST_CASE("FutexSpin-Timed-8bit") {
   uint8_t Test {};
 
-  auto now = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
+  auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
   FEXCore::Utils::SpinWaitLock::Wait(&Test, 1, SleepAmount);
-  auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
+  auto end = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
   auto diff = end - now;
 
-  // The futex spinwait needs to have slept for at /least/ the amount specified. It will always run slightly late.
-  REQUIRE(std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(diff) >= std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(SleepAmount));
+  // The futex spinwait needs to have slept for at /least/ the amount specified, less the cross-clock
+  // tolerance above. It will always run slightly late.
+  REQUIRE(std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(diff) >=
+          std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(SleepAmount - SleepTolerance));
 }
 
 TEST_CASE("FutexSpin-Sleep-8bit") {
@@ -26,10 +41,10 @@
   std::chrono::nanoseconds SleptAmount;
 
   std::thread t([&Test, &SleptAmount, &ActualSpinLoop]() {
-    auto now = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
+    auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
     ActualSpinLoop.store(1);
     FEXCore::Utils::SpinWaitLock::Wait(&Test, 1);
-    auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
+    auto end = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
     SleptAmount = end - now;
   });
 
@@ -53,35 +68,41 @@
